WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.420
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:09.869
In this series, we often marvel at light simulation
programs that are able to create beautiful

00:00:09.869 --> 00:00:14.629
images by simulating the path of millions
and millions of light rays.

00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:19.550
To make sure that our simulations look lifelike,
we not only have to make sure that these rays

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:24.380
of light interact with the geometry of the
scene in a way that is physically plausible,

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:29.869
but the materials within the simulation also
have to reflect reality.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:32.189
Now that’s an interesting problem.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:37.120
How do we create a convincing mathematical
description of real-world materials?

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.550
Well, one way to do that is taking a measurement
device, putting in a sample of the subject

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:47.440
material and measuring how rays of light bounce
off of it.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:53.730
This work introduces a new database for sophisticated
material models and includes interesting optical

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.990
effects, such as iridescence, which gives
the colorful physical appearance of bubbles

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:06.930
and fuel-water mixtures, it can do colorful
mirror-like specular highlights and more,

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:11.250
so we can include these materials in our light
simulation programs.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:16.960
You see this database in action in this scene
that showcases a collection of these complex

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.900
material models.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:24.940
However, creating such a database is not without
perils, because normally, these materials

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:30.000
take prohibitively many measurements to reproduce
properly, and the interesting regions are

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.159
often found at quite different places.

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:37.540
This paper proposes a solution that adapts
the location of these measurements to where

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:42.560
the action happens, resulting in a mathematical
description of these materials that can be

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.010
measured in a reasonable amount of time.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:49.850
It also takes very little memory when we run
the actual light simulation on them.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:55.000
So, as if light transport simulations weren’t
beautiful enough, they are about to get even

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.120
more realistic in the near future.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.150
Super excited for this.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:04.290
Make sure to have a look at the paper, which
is so good, I think I sank into a minor state

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.410
of shock upon reading it.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:10.979
If you are enjoying learning about light transport,
make sure to check out my course on this topic

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:12.969
at the Technical University of Vienna.

00:02:12.969 --> 00:02:18.810
I still teach this at the University for about
20 Master students at a time and thought that

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:23.909
the teachings shouldn't only be available
for a lucky few people who can afford a college

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:24.909
education.

00:02:24.909 --> 00:02:30.560
Clearly, the teachings should be available
for everyone, so, we recorded it and put it

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.110
online, and now everyone can watch it, free
of charge.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:39.760
I was quite stunned to see that more than
25 thousand people decided to start it, so

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.950
make sure to give it a go if you're interested!

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.689
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

